---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc920.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Program License Agreements — the Four Recognition Criteria" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Amortization of Program Rights" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Barter Transactions" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Network Affiliation Agreements" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Program License Asset/Liability Recognition" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — a Barter Transaction" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "No Dedicated IFRS Broadcaster Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">When does a licensed program right actually become an asset?</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Broadcasters commonly LICENSE programming content (movies, syndicated shows) from producers/distributors for airing over a specified period. A key question is WHEN the broadcaster should recognise the licensed program right as an ASSET (with a corresponding LIABILITY for amounts owed) — ASC 920 answers this with a specific FOUR-CRITERIA test (Section 3), reflecting that merely SIGNING a license agreement isn't itself sufficient.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Television and radio broadcasters</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">ASC 920 applies to entities operating television and radio BROADCAST stations and networks, addressing programming rights, barter arrangements, and network affiliation relationships specific to the broadcast industry.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Program License Agreements — the Four Recognition Criteria</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">ALL FOUR conditions must be met before recognising an asset/liability</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>Criterion</span><span>Requirement</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>1</span><span>The COST of the program is KNOWN or reasonably DETERMINABLE</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>2</span><span>The program has been ACCEPTED by the licensee (broadcaster) in accordance with the license agreement's terms (e.g. meets technical/content standards)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>3</span><span>The program is AVAILABLE for its FIRST SHOWING or telecast</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>4</span><span>The LICENSE PERIOD for the program has BEGUN</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Until ALL FOUR conditions are satisfied, NO asset or liability is recognised for the program license — even if the license agreement has been legally SIGNED.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Amortization of Program Rights</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Typically accelerated, reflecting greater value in earlier showings</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Program rights are AMORTISED to expense based on the PATTERN in which the program's economic benefits are expected to be CONSUMED — for programs with a limited number of permitted showings, this is often an ACCELERATED pattern (a GREATER portion of cost amortised against the FIRST showing than subsequent showings), reflecting that the FIRST airing typically commands the HIGHEST advertising value and audience interest.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Barter Transactions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Advertising time exchanged for programming or merchandise, at fair value</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">Broadcasters commonly engage in BARTER transactions — exchanging ADVERTISING TIME for PROGRAMMING content or merchandise/services, WITHOUT cash changing hands. Such transactions are measured at the FAIR VALUE of the assets/services received (or given, if more clearly evident) — the broadcaster records both BARTER REVENUE (or a program asset) and BARTER EXPENSE for the advertising time delivered, generally recognised as the related advertising airs or the programming is used.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Network Affiliation Agreements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Compensation arrangements between networks and local affiliate stations</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">NETWORK AFFILIATION agreements govern the relationship between a broadcast NETWORK and its local AFFILIATE stations — including compensation arrangements for carrying network programming, and any capitalisable costs associated with entering or renewing such agreements, accounted for under general intangible asset principles (ASC 350) where applicable.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Program License Asset/Liability Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Timing the recognition event</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Licensing a syndicated sitcom package</div><div class="wk-body">A broadcaster signs a license agreement in October for a $2,000,000 syndicated sitcom package. The programs are accepted (meeting technical standards) in November, but the license period doesn't begin — and the first show isn't available for airing — until January 1 of the following year.</div><div class="wk-answer">Even though the agreement was SIGNED in October and the programs ACCEPTED in November, NO asset or liability is recognised until <strong>January 1</strong> — the point at which ALL FOUR criteria (known cost, acceptance, availability for first showing, AND license period commencement) are simultaneously satisfied. At that point, the broadcaster recognises a <strong>$2,000,000 program rights asset</strong> and a corresponding <strong>$2,000,000 liability</strong>.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — a Barter Transaction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Measuring an exchange with no cash involved</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Trading airtime for syndicated programming</div><div class="wk-body">A local station exchanges $150,000 (fair value) of unsold advertising airtime for a syndicated game show package with an equivalent $150,000 fair value.</div><div class="wk-answer">The station records a <strong>$150,000 program rights asset</strong> (for the game show package received) and a corresponding <strong>$150,000 barter liability</strong> (representing the obligation to air the bartered advertising). As the advertising spots actually AIR, barter REVENUE of $150,000 is recognised, offset by barter EXPENSE as the program episodes are used — reflecting the fair value exchange even though no cash changed hands.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 920 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Basis of accounting for program licenses, including the amortisation method used</span><span>Nature and amount of barter transactions during the period</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether all four program license recognition criteria have been met</span><span>Appropriate amortisation pattern reflecting expected benefit consumption across showings</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of assets/services exchanged in barter transactions</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS Broadcaster Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">General intangible asset and inventory principles apply instead</div><div style="font-size: var(--text-small); color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">IFRS has NO dedicated broadcaster industry standard paralleling ASC 920. A broadcaster reporting under IFRS would apply GENERAL principles — recognising program rights as an INTANGIBLE ASSET (IAS 38) or INVENTORY (IAS 2) once control is obtained and future economic benefits are probable, amortised over the pattern of expected benefit consumption — arriving at BROADLY similar practical outcomes to ASC 920's four-criteria test and accelerated amortisation approach, but WITHOUT a codified, industry-specific four-part recognition test of the same kind.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 920 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Program license recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Asset and liability recognised once all four criteria are met</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Program rights amortisation</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Typically accelerated across showings</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Barter transaction</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised at fair value; revenue/expense as the exchange is used</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 926</span><span>Entertainment—Films — shares related cost capitalisation and amortisation concepts for licensed content</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general revenue recognition framework for advertising revenue and barter arrangements</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Program license agreement</div><div class="glossary-def">A contract granting a broadcaster the right to air licensed programming content for a specified period and number of showings.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Barter transaction</div><div class="glossary-def">An exchange of advertising airtime for programming or other goods/services, without cash, measured at fair value.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Network affiliation agreement</div><div class="glossary-def">A contract governing the relationship and compensation arrangement between a broadcast network and a local affiliate station.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc920 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>

